<h2>Setting up your calendar and availability</h2>

<p>Once you have chosen your software, the first step is configuring your availability. This is where you tell the system when you are open for bookings and what types of appointments you offer.</p>

<p><strong>Define your working hours.</strong> Set the days and hours when you are available. Be honest with yourself here. If you do not want to take clients before 9am or after 5pm, do not make those slots available. One of the benefits of online booking is that you control your calendar without having to negotiate with every customer individually.</p>

<p><strong>Create your service types.</strong> List each service you offer along with its duration and price. A haircut might be 30 minutes. A consultation might be 60 minutes. A deep-cleaning session might be 3 hours. Be specific about durations because your calendar relies on them to prevent overlapping bookings.</p>

<p><strong>Add buffer time between appointments.</strong> This is the detail that most people skip, and then regret. If you have back-to-back 60-minute appointments, you have zero time to clean up, prepare, take notes, eat lunch, or even use the bathroom. Add a 15 to 30 minute buffer between appointments. Your booking system should support this natively so that customers never see those buffer slots as available.</p>

<p><strong>Block off personal time and recurring commitments.</strong> Team meetings, lunch breaks, school pickup, gym sessions. Block them all. If it is on your calendar, it should not be bookable. Two-way calendar sync handles most of this automatically, but double-check that your recurring events are showing as blocked.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Set realistic working hours you can sustain long-term</li>
  <li>Create separate service types with accurate durations</li>
  <li>Add 15 to 30 minutes of buffer between appointments</li>
  <li>Block personal commitments and recurring events</li>
  <li>Set a maximum number of appointments per day if needed</li>
</ul>

<p>Some systems also let you set a maximum number of bookings per day. If you know that more than six clients in a day will burn you out, cap it at six. Your future self will thank you.</p>

