<h2>Automated confirmations and reminders</h2>

<p>This is where online booking starts paying for itself. Instead of manually sending appointment details and chasing confirmations, the system handles it all.</p>

<p><strong>The confirmation email.</strong> The moment a customer books, they should receive a confirmation email with the date, time, location or meeting link, any preparation instructions, and a clear option to reschedule or cancel. This is not just a receipt. It is the customer's first impression of your process, and a professional confirmation builds confidence that they made the right choice.</p>

<p><strong>The 24-hour reminder.</strong> One day before the appointment, an automated email goes out. This catches people who booked a week or two ago and may have forgotten. Include the same core details plus the reschedule link. Some people will realize they have a conflict, and giving them an easy way to reschedule is far better than a <a href="/blog/reduce-no-shows">no-show</a>.</p>

<p><strong>The 2-hour SMS.</strong> This is the most effective reminder in the sequence. A short, simple text message two hours before the appointment catches the people who do not check email, who forgot to add it to their personal calendar, or who just need a nudge. Keep it brief. "Hi Sarah, just a reminder about your appointment at 2pm today. Reply C to confirm or tap here to reschedule." Text messages have a 98% open rate compared to around 20% for email. If you are only going to set up one reminder, make it the SMS.</p>

<p>Set these up once and they run forever. Every new booking triggers the same sequence. No manual work. No forgotten reminders. No customers showing up on the wrong day because you forgot to send a confirmation.</p>

<blockquote><p>Automated reminders do not just reduce no-shows. They make your business feel organized and professional, which builds trust before the customer even walks through the door.</p></blockquote>

