<h2>Reducing no-shows</h2>

<p>Even with reminders, some people will miss their appointments. The strategies below work alongside your reminder sequence to bring that number as close to zero as possible.</p>

<p><strong>Require a deposit at booking.</strong> A deposit creates commitment. When someone has paid even $20, they are significantly more likely to show up or cancel properly rather than ghost you. For high-value services, a 50% deposit is standard and expected. For lower-value bookings, even a small amount makes a difference.</p>

<p><strong>Set a clear cancellation policy.</strong> State it on your booking page and reference it in your confirmation email. Something like "Cancel at least 24 hours before your appointment for a full refund of your deposit" is fair and widely accepted. The key is that people know about it before they book, not after they no-show.</p>

<p><strong>Make rescheduling effortless.</strong> Include a reschedule link in every reminder. One tap. No phone calls. No emails. The easier you make it to reschedule, the fewer people will simply not show up. A rescheduled appointment is still a booking. A no-show is a total loss.</p>

<p><strong>Add a confirmation step.</strong> Ask customers to confirm their appointment through your reminders. A simple "Reply C to confirm" or a confirmation button in the email creates a small psychological commitment that measurably reduces missed appointments.</p>

