<h2>Collecting payment at booking</h2>

<p>If you are not collecting payment or at least a deposit when someone books online, you are leaving money on the table and exposing yourself to no-shows.</p>

<p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> A booking without payment is really just a reservation of intent. There is no financial commitment, which means there is no friction to not showing up. Adding payment at the point of booking solves two problems at once: it reduces no-shows and it eliminates the awkward "collecting payment after the service" moment.</p>

<p><strong>Deposit vs. full payment.</strong> This depends on your service and price point. For services under $100, collecting the full amount at booking is common and expected. For higher-value services, a deposit of 25% to 50% is standard. Some businesses offer both options, letting the customer choose to pay in full for a small discount or pay a deposit now and the balance later.</p>

<p><strong>Reducing payment friction.</strong> Accept the payment methods your customers prefer. Credit and debit cards are the baseline. Apple Pay and Google Pay should be available if your payment processor supports them. The more options you offer, the fewer people will abandon the booking at the payment step. Keep the payment form minimal. Name, card number, and a pay button. Do not ask for billing addresses or secondary information unless your processor requires it.</p>

<p><strong>Refund and cancellation clarity.</strong> When you collect payment at booking, you need a clear refund policy attached to your cancellation policy. This does not need to be complicated. "Full refund for cancellations 24 hours or more before your appointment. No refund for cancellations within 24 hours or no-shows." Display this clearly before the payment step so there are no surprises.</p>

